    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. how does the body actually handle the nitrogen surplus when we're in that caloric surplus state? i want to make sure i explain the metabolic pathway for disposing of the excess nitrogen so they don't think it's just being 'stored' as muscle.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When you're in a caloric surplus, and you're providing a nitrogen surplus from adequate protein intake, the body doesn't just magically turn all that excess nitrogen into muscle; it has highly regulated metabolic pathways for handling it. The excess amino acids that aren't immediately used for protein synthesis are deaminated, meaning the nitrogen group is removed from the amino acid structure. This removed nitrogen is then primarily converted into urea via the urea cycle, which is a detoxification process carried out mainly by the liver. This urea is then excreted by the kidneys, effectively disposing of the excess nitrogen safely.",
      "source": "target",
